    "title": "knightmotives_automotive: Safe Phased OTA Firmware Rollouts Across 200,000 Tractors",
    "scenario": "knightmotives_automotive is pushing a critical ECU engine control firmware update to 200,000 tractors globally. If a firmware bug causes tractors to stall, agricultural operations could halt across thousands of farms. How should the OTA deployment pipeline be structured to maximize safety and minimize blast radius?",
    "keywords": [
      "knightmotives_automotive",
      "OTA Firmware",
      "Phased Rollout",
      "Blast Radius Reduction",
      "Canary Deployment",
      "Automated Pause"
    ],
    "isMultiSelect": false,
    "expectedSelectCount": 1,
    "options": [
      {
        "letter": "A",
        "text": "Ship the over-the-air firmware to every tractor at once but write it to the inactive A/B partition, leave the fleet on the current image for a 24h soak, then flip all 200,000 vehicles to the new partition and roll back the whole fleet together if failures appear."
      },
      {
        "letter": "B",
        "text": "Run a fifty-fifty split test across the global fleet, giving half the tractors the new firmware and half the current one in a side-by-side comparison, and have the reliability team compare the two cohorts after 14 days of observation before deciding whether to move the remainder."
      },
      {
        "letter": "C",
        "text": "Execute a phased progressive rollout: Stage 1: 100 internal test tractors -> Stage 2: 1% dealer loaner fleet -> Stage 3: 10% regional fleet -> Stage 4: 100% global fleet; monitor tractor CAN-bus error telemetry in BigQuery in real-time and automatically halt rollout if error rates exceed 0.1%."
      },
      {
        "letter": "D",
        "text": "Roll out region by region with a manual go/no-go from the release manager between regions, using dealer warranty claims and service-centre tickets from the previous region as the health signal and pausing two weeks between regions so those claims have time to accumulate."
      }
    ],
    "correct": "C",
    "explanation": "Phased canary rollouts for IoT/automotive firmware limit failure blast radius. Telemetry is monitored after each stage, with automated halt thresholds preventing buggy firmware from spreading to the global fleet.",
    "distractors": {
      "D": "Warranty claims and service tickets are lagging indicators that surface days or weeks after the failure, so by the time the first region's claims arrive the release manager has already approved the following regions. The halt signal must come from real-time vehicle telemetry.",
      "B": "Un split test cincuenta-cincuenta expone de entrada a la mitad de los 200.000 tractores al firmware nuevo, sin ninguna etapa previa de bajo riesgo (cien unidades internas, luego un 1%); si el bug es grave, el radio de impacto inicial ya es enorme.",
      "A": "Actualizar el firmware de los 200.000 tractores a la vez (aunque sea a la particion A/B inactiva) no limita el radio de impacto: si el bug aparece durante el soak de 24h, todo el parque agricola global ya recibio el firmware defectuoso, justo lo que el enunciado pide evitar con un rollout progresivo."
    },
